  {
   "heading": "Specify case bins",
   "segment_index": "S8.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:182-182",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.jp.qmd:170-170",
   "proposition": "The English CAUTION states that the first 7-day bin created by binwidth = 7 can start on any day of the week; the Japanese caution says only that the first bin starts at the first case and drops the 'any day of the week' point.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Japanese reader does not learn that binwidth = 7 bins are anchored to an arbitrary weekday, so they may present binwidth = 7 bars as if they were standard Monday (or epidemiological) weeks.",
   "confidence": "medium"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Specify case bins",
   "segment_index": "S8.P2",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:201-201",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.jp.qmd:188-188",
   "proposition": "The Japanese states that the first case in the Central Hospital dataset had symptom onset on 'day 1' (1 日目); the English makes no such claim, it opens a sentence that the following code completes by printing the actual weekday and date.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Japanese reader is told a fabricated fact about the data and cannot connect the sentence to the printed value of format(min(central_data$date_onset), '%A %d %b, %Y'), which is a full weekday-date, not 'day 1'.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Weekly epicurve example",
   "segment_index": "S9.P1",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:280-280",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.jp.qmd:268-268",
   "proposition": "The Japanese says closed = \"left\" is used to check that the date LABELS are counted into the correct bins; the English says it ensures the DATES (the case observations) are counted in the correct bins.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Japanese reader misunderstands closed = \"left\" as an axis-labelling control rather than a bin-boundary rule for case counting, which contradicts the same chapter's later statement that date labels are independent of the data aggregation.",
   "confidence": "medium"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Bars side-by-side",
   "segment_index": "S15.P1",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:538-538",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.jp.qmd:513-513",
   "proposition": "The Japanese sets the readability threshold at two or more groups (2 つ以上のグループがある場合); the English sets it at more than two groups.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Japanese reader is told that even two side-by-side groups become hard to read, contradicting the example immediately below, which uses exactly two groups (gender) as a working dodge plot, and may switch to faceting when dodging is fine.",
